orld of difference in terms of how  the image rendered and the colors just  came alive so a little tip for you guys  on that  shoot in spot mode and uh yeah  auto exposure lock it does slow you down  a little bit  but  the end result is worth it  all right guys we're back in the home  studio let's take a look at images from  the hasselblad 907x plus the 3575 3.5 to  4.5 xcd lens  let's look at them now before i get into  images that i've taken at vicious cycles  i want to show you some images from my  friend peter chen as i mentioned earlier  in this review he was very kind enough  to loan me uh these images to show you  guys and this is from a walk that we did  together with the 907x he's never used  this camera before  he's used to using the old v series  hasselblad camera when he was doing  product photography um earlier in his  career so he was excited to try out this  907x so i said let's give it a shot and  these are some of the results  from one day actually one afternoon with  this camera  very very impressive and they're also a  very good testament to how good this  lens is case in point we were in the  central business district area in  singapore which is beautiful there's a  lot of you know high rises and in this  area there's a lot of blue towers that  are just it's really nice to see  but  i mean people could shoot buildings and  it can look plain or it can look very  interesting and i love how i love how  peter captured this case in point he  found this angle that you could see the  light and the dark to it and just this  depth to the buildings which was  beautiful to see but when we look at it  from a you know lens perspective look  how sharp it is here in the corners  all the way  to each corner here  to the center and he didn't do any  correction in terms of lens correction  in photoshop with this he just really  just tweaked the colors a little bit but  this is how it looks out of camera but  then he went to black and white  and black and white  adds a whole other dimension to it it is  absolutely beautiful  look at the tonality from the dark to  the light the graduation in the from the  you know  from the shadows to the exposed areas  it's just  something that medium format gives you i  mean you guys know this if you shoot  medium format i mean can you get some of  this from full frame  you can but it's not the same honestly  speaking yes 14 stops dynamic range you  can look at all the numbers it can be  very similar  to full frame  but when you see it up close you see  that the depth and the how organic the  image looks  it's what makes medium format so special  and of course it's a testament to this  lens how sharp it is there's no fringing  to this whatsoever the color  reproduction the sensor all of this  combined really makes for  beautiful images here's another shot of  this dbs tower here you can see the blue  reflections there and he preferred the  black and white and i do too because of  how dramatic it can look  but look at the tonality there and if we  just kind of you know punch in on this  you know how sharp it is give it a  second to render again these are about  300 megabyte images  and in  lightroom you know obviously it does  take a little bit of time  to uh pull up but we look at the image  as a whole how beautiful it looks i mean  unbelievable  um really really impressive i mean you  can just see the details in the windows  you can even see in some of the offices  there the lights they're showcasing  there it's really really something to  see but let's look at another image here  um from this shoot i did with a friend  of mine he has a mercedes sls beautiful  car  and uh this is the original image these  spots here are dust spots that were on  the sensor because the sensor can get  quite dirty if you remove the back off  and on so do take note of that get some  you know sensor cleaning uh products if  you have if you can get them  but uh unfortunately i didn't bring any  strobe light to this i was using natural  lighting the light was behind the car  so i really had to rely on the dynamic  range now the next image i'm going to  show you has been  it's been highly edited it's more  dramatic it might not be to your liking  but the owner of the vehicle wanted the  image to look this way so you know we do  what we're asked to do and here's the  end result so i did use a series of um  programs for this obviously lightroom  photoshop luminar ai  even focus for that matter to sort of do  the raw conversion initially um but yeah  it's  a little dramatic some people love it  but i wanted to give it that that uh  that pop and as you can tell just the  detail on there it still maintains of  course i uh  you know  what from the original to this  it's a testament to the dynamic range of  the sensor and again how good the lens  is because it is sharp to sharp it's  it's sharp corner to corner on this  really really impressive  so let's take a look at this image of  andre who owns painful industries inside  vicious cycles and he wanted a portrait  of himself but unfortunately i didn't  bring a strobe light again happens to me  quite a bit but we're in a motorcycle  shop i don't want to bring a lot of  equipment in there's a lot of things  that can you know mess things up so i  was using the lighting sources that were  available to me and there was two  polarizing lighting sources on one side  there's this bright white it almost has  a bluish  tint to it then on the other side this  really warm orange light and i'm trying  to balance in between them and i try to  do the best as i could and i'll show you  what i'm talking about here is the  original image and it is a little bit  more warmer than i wanted and it just  wasn't working for me of course i did  play with the the colors a bit in the  lightroom and i was like get to this  more of this um  you know kind of toned them down a  little bit but i brought this more  natural organic look to it but it's just  the tonality that you get from the  sensor that really makes it even  interesting  and then this is shot at iso 3200 and as  we look uh you know behind him here you  can see the grain here it's not  distracting in any way as a matter of  fact it looks more fill mask but some  people don't want any grain anyway  with  you know a little bit of topaz de noise  i was able to clean that up and really  get a much cleaner image out of that  while still maintaining the detail in  the face so um i just started using this  software mind you other people use it  friends of mine use it do do wildlife  and birding and they swear by it and i  just tried it out and i can attest that  it really is  worth  a download to try it out to buy it or  not is up to you guys but it is  definitely worth trying it out but i  just again love the tonality and just  something that medium format gives you  especially in portraiture that is really  really unique um here's another shot of  andre and this is uh behind here and  look at all the detail that we're able  to capture on his back and he's trying  all this lint and everything else now  the final image that you have guys seen  in the review i've removed all that and  cleaned it up but i mean it's just quite  amazing how sharp this lens is but  what about chromatic aberration what  about purple or green fringing when it  comes to metallic objects let's take a  look at something right now here's um  these shocks that were on the wall here  at vicious cycle and again this is a  testament to how good this lens is  is i'm looking at these these shocks  here and they're chrome there's rust  there's all different tonalities and  colors and textures and i'm not seeing  anything that's distracting whatsoever  and yes this image has been edited let  me show the before edit  let's zoom in on that  look how control that is  i can't see anything honestly and here's  after edit  before edit  and after edit  and again  edge to edge  is sharp  very very impressive  uh let's look at this on the side of  this motorcycle real quick  i have this old honda here that's been  converted to a sort of a a chopper of  sorts gotta customize have some fun with  it but again look at this area right  here uh it's just beautiful i mean yeah  it's rust there's texture to it but if  you see how well the colors and the  tonality that just comes alive from the  sensor and the lens  and how much depth there is to this it  really is something to see  this is before edit and here's after  edit before editing after it now and  it's our personal preference you may  like or dislike what i'm doing but it is  something to take note of on it just uh  in terms of its texture  and also here's a tank that andre was  working on i think he's just finished it  but uh he just added these skulls on to  it here and he was just you know  brushing it up here on this area but  look at the  texture and the detail on this  um this is an iso 3200 mind you and it  is absolutely  beautiful at iso 3200 in terms of this  detail so again something to really take  note of in terms of performance of this  uh  this camera and this lens and here's one  last shot i want to show you guys this  is of this red harley-davidson v-raw  tank that he painted recently this is  deep cherry red and when i saw it in  person it just it just stood out to me  the tonality the depth to it and you  know yes with full frame cameras you can  capture some of this but that's where  medium format comes into play that's why  a lot of people still use medium format  for product photography to get that  tonality those colors that 16-bit color  you know that that dynamic range that  you get you see from the light to the  dark it's just that graduation it's  beautiful now this has been edited  slightly but  not much to mind you i mean most of this  was done with um spot metering and auto  exposure lock to kind of capture what i  was seeing with my eye because sometimes  if you just let the camera do every